capability x509/chain-build  a chain put in order from a leaf and a pool
  heroes  x509.chain_of
  go      x509.Certificate.Verify, x509.NewCertPool, x509.CertPool, x509.CertPool.AddCert, x509.CertPool.AddCertWithConstraint, x509.CertPool.Clone, x509.CertPool.Equal, x509.CertPool.Subjects
  rust    webpki::EndEntityCert::verify_for_usage, webpki::VerifiedPath
  python  x509.verification.PolicyBuilder
  note    the pool is a list, as a program holds it; an issuer chosen by name, key identifier and a signature that holds, greedily, never by backtracking: of two issuers that both sign, the first whose signature holds is taken, where Go's `Verify` builds "one or more chains" (its documentation)

capability x509/chain-verify  a chain checked: names, signatures, validity, authority, path length, key usage, critical extensions, name constraints, purpose, host
  heroes  x509.verify_chain, x509.Verification
  go      x509.Certificate.Verify, x509.VerifyOptions, x509.CertificateInvalidError, x509.InvalidReason, x509.UnknownAuthorityError, x509.ConstraintViolationError, x509.HostnameError, x509.SystemRootsError
  rust    webpki::EndEntityCert::verify_for_usage, webpki::KeyUsage, webpki::RequiredEkuNotFoundContext
  python  x509.verification.PolicyBuilder, x509.verification.Store, x509.verification.ServerVerifier, x509.verification.ClientVerifier, x509.verification.VerificationError
  note    Go's and Python's answers on the samples held: the chain at its moment, after it and without its intermediate; a code-signing purpose; a host it is not for; a leaf the excluded `bad.example.com` covers, one outside the permitted DNS names, one outside the permitted addresses. Name constraints are checked on DNS names (a wildcard excluded where any name it stands for would be, as both refuse it), email addresses, URIs' hosts, addresses and directory names; an otherName's are not. Revocation is not asked: `revoked_of` answers for a list a program fetched

capability x509/policy-processing  RFC 5280 § 6.1's processing of certificate policies over a chain
  heroes  pending
  go      x509.VerifyOptions (CertificatePolicies)
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the policies are read; their tree over a chain is not built, so `verify_chain` answers as if every policy were acceptable

capability x509/host  whether a certificate is for a host name, as RFC 6125 says
  heroes  x509.is_for_host
  go      x509.Certificate.VerifyHostname
  rust    webpki::EndEntityCert::verify_is_valid_for_subject_name, rustls_pki_types::ServerName, rustls_pki_types::DnsName
  python  -
  note    Go's answers on twenty-three hosts held: case and one trailing dot ignored, a wildcard the whole first label and one label alone, an IP literal matched by address, an IPv4 address and the IPv6 one mapping it one. A host holding a `*` is no host, where Go matches `*.example.com` against the same pattern (observed); the common name is never read, as RFC 9525 and Go read none. Python 3.15's `ssl` holds no `match_hostname` (its names listed)

capability x509/address  whether a certificate is for an IP address
  heroes  x509.is_for_address
  go      x509.Certificate.VerifyHostname
  rust    rustls_pki_types::IpAddr
