capability x509/system-roots  the system's own store of trusted roots read
  heroes  pending
  go      x509.SystemCertPool, x509.SetFallbackRoots, x509.SystemRootsError
  rust    rustls_native_certs::load_native_certs
  python  ssl.SSLContext.load_default_certs, ssl.get_default_verify_paths
  note    `tls` checks a connection by the system's store; reading the store's certificates needs a floor on each platform's own store

capability x509/create-certificate  a certificate made and signed by an authority for a key
  heroes  x509.create_certificate
  go      x509.CreateCertificate
  rust    -
  python  x509.CertificateBuilder
  note    the signature is `keypairs.sign`'s, its scheme the caller's and never chosen for the key; the structure is `asn1`'s, written as OpenSSL 4.1.0 writes it (the tests compare the bytes of an Ed25519 leaf and an RSA certificate with OpenSSL's, 2026-11-23, and `openssl verify` accepts each scheme's chain). What is made is read back and its signature checked. The serial is positive and 20 octets at most in DER, as Python refuses one of 160 bits (observed on `cryptography` 50.0.2); a validity may end where it starts, Python's `not_valid_after` being asked after the start; an empty subject with no alternative name is refused, which Python makes. The issuer's name is its subject's own bytes, its key identifier the authority key identifier's. The extensions of other documents go through `Profile`'s `extra`, written as given

capability x509/create-self-signed  a certificate a key gives itself: a root authority, a server's own certificate
  heroes  x509.create_self_signed
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Go's `CreateCertificate` with the template as its own parent and Python's builder with one name for both make it, and no function of theirs says so; no authority key identifier is written, as RFC 5280 § 4.2.1.1 allows

capability x509/create-extensions  the extensions a certificate or a request writes from fields: the role, the key's usages and purposes, the subject's names
  heroes  x509.profile_extensions
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `basicConstraints` and `keyUsage` are written critical, the alternative names critical only where the subject is empty. Refused where RFC 5280 forbids it: `keyCertSign` with no authority, an authority's `keyUsage` without it, `encipherOnly` without `keyAgreement` (Python refuses the last as well, observed). A name that is an address is `.ip`, never a `.dns`; a `.dns` of text that parses as an address is refused

capability x509/create-name  a name and its attributes made from text
  heroes  x509.new_attribute
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the string type is RFC 5280's: PrintableString for a country and a serial number, IA5String for an email address and a domain component, UTF8String for the rest, as OpenSSL 4.1.0 writes them with `string_mask = utf8only`; the bounds of Appendix A are counted in characters. `x509.new_name` makes the name of its attributes, the relative names in DER's order

capability x509/create-request  a certificate request made and signed
  heroes  x509.create_request
  go      x509.CreateCertificateRequest
  rust    -
  python  x509.CertificateSigningRequestBuilder
  note    the tests compare the bytes of an Ed25519 request with OpenSSL's (`openssl req -new`, 4.1.0, 2026-11-23), and `openssl req -verify` accepts each scheme's. The profile is written as the `extensionRequest` attribute; other attributes (`challengePassword`) are given whole, each with a value at least, an `extensionRequest` among them refused. What is made is read back and its signature checked

capability x509/create-crl  a revocation list made and signed by its issuer
  heroes  x509.create_crl
  go      x509.CreateRevocationList, x509.Certificate.CreateCRL
  rust    -
  python  x509.CertificateRevocationListBuilder, x509.RevokedCertificateBuilder
  note    version 2, with `authorityKeyIdentifier` and `cRLNumber`, each entry's reason and invalidity date; the tests compare the bytes of an Ed25519 list of three entries with `openssl ca -gencrl`'s (4.1.0, 2026-11-23). An entry's `unspecified` reason writes none, as RFC 5280 § 5.3.1 asks, and `removeFromCRL` is refused, delta lists and indirect ones being unwritten. The issuer's certificate must allow `cRLSign` where it states its usages

